      "subfigures": 1,
      "observations": [
        {
          "id": "fig-213-source-and-slit",
          "source_fact": "One star-like light source near (397,27), above two horizontal bars near y=69. The bars leave a central gap on the vertical incident ray. This is a slit/aperture symbol, not a separately drawn convex lens. A downward direction marker occurs below it."
        },
        {
          "id": "fig-213-octagonal-prism",
          "source_fact": "One eight-sided outline at right, approximately x=383..486 and y=134..238. The top and bottom sides are horizontal; the left and right middle sides are vertical; four corner facets are sloping. A double circular spindle/hub is inside. The vertical incident line meets the upper-left sloping facet near (397,149); the lower horizontal returning line meets the lower-left sloping facet near (397,225). A curved rotation indication inside the upper-left portion goes toward the lower left. No angular amount, face numbering, speed, or rotation-period value is printed."
        },
        {
          "id": "fig-213-far-reflectors",
          "source_fact": "A tall concave reflector at left has its reflective side facing right and hatching behind it on the left; its ends flare right. A smaller vertical plane reflector near x=132, y=160..214 has hatching to its right and its reflective face toward the concave reflector. Upper and lower reflection spots on the concave reflector connect diagonally to the same middle spot on the plane reflector."
        },
        {
          "id": "fig-213-complete-directed-path",
          "source_fact": "Read the entire connected path, not just its two long horizontal legs: light source -> slit -> upper-left prism facet (down); upper prism facet -> upper concave-mirror spot (left); upper concave spot -> small plane mirror (down/right); plane mirror -> lower concave spot (down/left); lower concave spot -> lower-left prism facet (right); lower prism facet -> telescope entrance (down). Both long horizontal legs have an interrupted/dashed middle section indicating the distant span, with opposite arrow directions. The internal left-hand diagonal legs have their own direction markers. There are no extra beam splitters, second distant apparatus, or separate subpanels."
        },
        {
          "id": "fig-213-telescope",
          "source_fact": "A vertical telescope lies below the prism on the returning vertical line, around x=380..420, y=300..494. It has a top entrance/objective end, a long tube, three conspicuous dark collar/band regions around y=310,360,442, and a lower narrowed/flared end. The written question identifies an eyepiece. The source's heavy black tube does not resolve individual internal lens surfaces or their count; do not claim three lenses merely from three dark bands."
        },
        {
          "id": "fig-213-labels-and-givens",
          "source_fact": "The cropped figure has no readable word labels, numbers, distances, lens identifiers, or subfigure letters. The distance and octagonal-prism identity come from exercise 1, not printed pixel labels."
        }
      ],
      "modern_additions": [
        "English apparatus labels identify the existing source, slit, concave mirror, plane mirror, octagonal prism, telescope objective and eyepiece. They are modern explanatory labels, not translations of nonexistent source lettering.",
